From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tamnd <tamnd@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 22:45:21 +0800
Subject: bazel: add a windows x86_64 target platform

There is no `@platforms//os:windows` anywhere in the tree, so a Windows targeted
build cannot be asked for at all. Every OS keyed `select()` is written against
Linux and macOS, and there is nothing for a fourth arm to resolve against.

Add the two things that everything else will need. A `config_setting` named
`windows_x86_64`, next to `linux_x86_64` and `linux_aarch64` and matching their
naming, so that `select()` sites can key on it the same way. And a `platform()`
so that `--platforms=//:windows-x86_64-platform` means something.

The platform sits above the existing `platform()` rules but it is a different
kind of thing to them, which the comment says out loud: those describe remote
execution workers and carry `exec_properties`, this one describes a target and
carries none. Execution stays on whatever machine is running the build, which is
what makes this a cross compile and is what keeps the toolchain driver scripts,
which are bash, viable for a Windows target.

Naming. The `config_setting` gets the underscored name because that is the
convention its neighbours follow and because it is the name that will be repeated
across a lot of `select()` arms later. The platform cannot also be called that,
since target names are unique within a package, so it takes the `-platform`
suffix the other `platform()` rules already use.

Nothing builds for this yet. A build asking for it fails, which is the point:
before this the request could not be expressed, so the failure was much further
downstream and much less clear.

Signed-off-by: tamnd <1218621+tamnd@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 BUILD.bazel | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/BUILD.bazel b/BUILD.bazel
index 6c8833f008..c8ac8f40a4 100644
--- a/BUILD.bazel
+++ b/BUILD.bazel
@@ -201,6 +201,15 @@ config_setting(
     ],
 )
 
+config_setting(
+    name = "windows_x86_64",
+    constraint_values = [
+        "@platforms//cpu:x86_64",
+        "@platforms//os:windows",
+    ],
+    tags = [ALLOW_UNUSED_TAG],
+)
+
 alias(
     name = "lint",
     actual = "//bazel/lint",
@@ -225,6 +234,19 @@ alias(
     tags = [TOP_LEVEL_TAG],
 )
 
+# A target platform, unlike the ones below it, which are execution platforms for
+# remote workers. Nothing builds for it yet. It exists so that a Windows targeted
+# build can be asked for at all, with --platforms=//:windows-x86_64-platform, and
+# so that the OS keyed selects in the tree have something to resolve against.
+# Execution stays wherever the build is being run, so this cross compiles.
+platform(
+    name = "windows-x86_64-platform",
+    constraint_values = [
+        "@platforms//cpu:x86_64",
+        "@platforms//os:windows",
+    ],
+)
+
 platform(
     name = "m7i-platform",
     constraint_values = [
